1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,222
Imagine the last plane of the season takes off,

2
00:00:03,222 --> 00:00:05,827
and you're not on it. For the next six

3
00:00:05,827 --> 00:00:08,500
months, no one can come to get you. The

4
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:12,202
Sun is about to disappear, and the temperature outside

5
00:00:12,202 --> 00:00:15,561
will drop lower than anywhere else on Earth. This

6
00:00:15,561 --> 00:00:18,920
is winter in Antarctica. How long would you last?

7
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:21,662
Stay to the end to find out who actually

8
00:00:21,662 --> 00:00:23,787
survives it, every single year.

9
00:00:24,337 --> 00:00:27,758
Welcome to Earth Decoded. If you love the most

10
00:00:27,758 --> 00:00:31,998
extreme places on our planet, tap subscribe. Today, we're

11
00:00:31,998 --> 00:00:35,345
spending a winter at the bottom of the world.

12
00:00:35,895 --> 00:00:40,474
Antarctica is the coldest, driest and windiest continent. In

13
00:00:40,474 --> 00:00:44,671
1983, at Russia's Vostok Station, deep in the interior,

14
00:00:44,671 --> 00:00:48,563
the air reached minus eighty nine point two degrees

15
00:00:48,563 --> 00:00:53,600
Celsius. That's minus one hundred twenty eight degrees Fahrenheit,

16
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:58,103
the coldest temperature ever recorded by a weather station.

17
00:00:58,653 --> 00:01:02,269
Satellites have measured even colder spots on the high

18
00:01:02,269 --> 00:01:05,952
ice plateau, close to minus one hundred degrees Celsius

19
00:01:05,952 --> 00:01:08,765
at the surface during clear winter nights.

20
00:01:09,315 --> 00:01:12,597
Why so cold? Antarctica is a giant dome of

21
00:01:12,597 --> 00:01:16,504
ice, more than two kilometers thick on average. Up

22
00:01:16,504 --> 00:01:19,474
high, the air is thin and dry. And the

23
00:01:19,474 --> 00:01:23,538
bright white snow reflects most of the sunlight back

24
00:01:23,538 --> 00:01:24,397
into space.

25
00:01:24,947 --> 00:01:28,216
And in winter, there's no sunlight to reflect at

26
00:01:28,216 --> 00:01:28,489
all.

27
00:01:29,039 --> 00:01:32,015
At the South Pole, the Sun sets around March

28
00:01:32,015 --> 00:01:36,005
and doesn't rise again until around September. That's about

29
00:01:36,005 --> 00:01:39,252
six months of night. For weeks, there isn't even

30
00:01:39,252 --> 00:01:43,243
twilight, just stars, the Moon, and the shimmering southern

31
00:01:43,243 --> 00:01:43,716
lights.

32
00:01:44,266 --> 00:01:48,140
Living in darkness messes with your body clock. People

33
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:51,943
have trouble sleeping, feel tired and moody, and lose

34
00:01:51,943 --> 00:01:55,028
track of the days. Doctors even have a name

35
00:01:55,028 --> 00:01:57,109
for it: winter-over syndrome.

36
00:01:57,659 --> 00:02:01,257
Quick question. Could you handle months without seeing the

37
00:02:01,257 --> 00:02:03,056
Sun? Tell me in the comments.

38
00:02:03,606 --> 00:02:06,897
So what happens if you step outside without the

39
00:02:06,897 --> 00:02:10,679
right gear? At minus seventy degrees, exposed skin can

40
00:02:10,679 --> 00:02:14,320
freeze in less than a minute. Your eyelashes freeze,

41
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:17,401
and the moisture in your breath turns to ice

42
00:02:17,401 --> 00:02:19,222
crystals around your face.

43
00:02:19,772 --> 00:02:22,973
Breathing that air hurts. It's so cold and dry

44
00:02:22,973 --> 00:02:26,244
that it can sting your throat and lungs. People

45
00:02:26,244 --> 00:02:29,863
cover their mouths with scarves to warm each breath.

46
00:02:30,413 --> 00:02:34,292
Then comes hypothermia. Your body shivers hard to make

47
00:02:34,292 --> 00:02:38,028
heat. As your core temperature drops, you get clumsy

48
00:02:38,028 --> 00:02:41,979
and confused. Without shelter, it doesn't take long for

49
00:02:41,979 --> 00:02:43,703
things to become deadly.

50
00:02:44,253 --> 00:02:48,433
The wind makes everything worse. Near the coast, katabatic

51
00:02:48,433 --> 00:02:51,388
winds pour down off the ice dome. In some

52
00:02:51,388 --> 00:02:54,703
places, they can blow at more than two hundred

53
00:02:54,703 --> 00:02:56,072
kilometers an hour.

54
00:02:56,622 --> 00:02:59,952
In winter, almost no plane can land. It's too

55
00:02:59,952 --> 00:03:03,578
cold for normal fuel and hydraulics, and too dark

56
00:03:03,578 --> 00:03:07,056
to see. If someone gets sick, they usually have

57
00:03:07,056 --> 00:03:08,462
to wait for spring.

58
00:03:09,012 --> 00:03:12,945
There have been rare emergency flights. In 1999, a

59
00:03:12,945 --> 00:03:16,170
doctor at the South Pole had to treat her

60
00:03:16,170 --> 00:03:20,260
own cancer, with supplies dropped by a plane, before

61
00:03:20,260 --> 00:03:23,092
she could be flown out months later.

62
00:03:23,642 --> 00:03:27,401
In 1961, at a Soviet station, the only doctor

63
00:03:27,401 --> 00:03:31,495
got appendicitis. With no way out, he removed his

64
00:03:31,495 --> 00:03:36,257
own appendix, using a mirror, while two colleagues helped

65
00:03:36,257 --> 00:03:36,591
him.

66
00:03:37,141 --> 00:03:40,959
People have paid a terrible price to learn these

67
00:03:40,959 --> 00:03:44,379
lessons. In 1911, two teams raced to be the

68
00:03:44,379 --> 00:03:48,117
first to reach the South Pole: a Norwegian team

69
00:03:48,117 --> 00:03:51,696
led by Roald Amundsen, and a British team led

70
00:03:51,696 --> 00:03:53,525
by Robert Falcon Scott.

71
00:03:54,075 --> 00:03:58,226
Amundsen used sled dogs and skis, and reached the

72
00:03:58,226 --> 00:04:03,394
Pole on December fourteenth, 1911. Scott's team arrived about

73
00:04:03,394 --> 00:04:07,545
five weeks later, only to find the Norwegian flag

74
00:04:07,545 --> 00:04:08,731
already there.

75
00:04:09,281 --> 00:04:12,586
On the way back, Scott and his four companions

76
00:04:12,586 --> 00:04:16,105
were caught by brutal cold and blizzards. None of

77
00:04:16,105 --> 00:04:19,984
them survived. Their tent was found months later, just

78
00:04:19,984 --> 00:04:22,785
about eleven miles from a supply depot.

79
00:04:23,335 --> 00:04:27,421
A few years later, Ernest Shackleton's ship Endurance was

80
00:04:27,421 --> 00:04:30,790
crushed by sea ice. His men survived for almost

81
00:04:30,790 --> 00:04:33,370
two years on the ice and on a remote

82
00:04:33,370 --> 00:04:36,524
island, and every single one of them made it

83
00:04:36,524 --> 00:04:36,882
home.

84
00:04:37,432 --> 00:04:41,522
In 2022, the wreck of Endurance was finally found,

85
00:04:41,522 --> 00:04:46,185
three thousand meters deep in the Weddell Sea, remarkably

86
00:04:46,185 --> 00:04:48,803
well preserved by the icy water.

87
00:04:49,353 --> 00:04:53,212
And yet, every winter, around a thousand people stay

88
00:04:53,212 --> 00:04:58,110
in Antarctica on purpose. Scientists, engineers, cooks and doctors

89
00:04:58,110 --> 00:05:01,598
live at research stations across the continent.

90
00:05:02,148 --> 00:05:05,228
At the Amundsen Scott South Pole Station, a few

91
00:05:05,228 --> 00:05:08,505
dozen people spend the winter in a raised building

92
00:05:08,505 --> 00:05:11,716
that looks like a spaceship, sitting right at the

93
00:05:11,716 --> 00:05:13,092
bottom of the planet.

94
00:05:13,642 --> 00:05:17,534
There's a famous tradition there called the three hundred

95
00:05:17,534 --> 00:05:21,358
club. When it hits minus one hundred degrees Fahrenheit,

96
00:05:21,358 --> 00:05:24,294
people sit in a sauna heated to two hundred

97
00:05:24,294 --> 00:05:27,504
degrees, then run outside to the pole marker, a

98
00:05:27,504 --> 00:05:29,621
swing of three hundred degrees.

99
00:05:30,171 --> 00:05:33,591
In the middle of the ice plateau, the French

100
00:05:33,591 --> 00:05:38,099
Italian Concordia Station is so isolated that the European

101
00:05:38,099 --> 00:05:41,986
Space Agency uses it to study how astronauts might

102
00:05:41,986 --> 00:05:45,017
cope on a mission to Mars. They call it

103
00:05:45,017 --> 00:05:45,872
White Mars.

104
00:05:46,422 --> 00:05:49,911
Would you volunteer for a winter at White Mars?

105
00:05:49,911 --> 00:05:51,841
Yes or no in the comments.

106
00:05:52,391 --> 00:05:55,681
How do they get through it? Stations grow fresh

107
00:05:55,681 --> 00:06:00,511
vegetables in greenhouses under lamps. People celebrate Midwinter Day

108
00:06:00,511 --> 00:06:03,522
in June with a big feast, and stations even

109
00:06:03,522 --> 00:06:06,812
send each other greetings across the continent.

110
00:06:07,362 --> 00:06:10,898
They keep busy with science. In the clean, dry

111
00:06:10,898 --> 00:06:14,741
winter air, telescopes at the South Pole study the

112
00:06:14,741 --> 00:06:18,507
oldest light in the universe, and a huge detector

113
00:06:18,507 --> 00:06:22,503
buried in the ice, IceCube, hunts for tiny particles

114
00:06:22,503 --> 00:06:23,810
called neutrinos.

115
00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:27,694
And there's a reward for those who stay. During

116
00:06:27,694 --> 00:06:31,100
the long polar night, the sky over Antarctica is

117
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:34,647
often lit up by the aurora australis, the southern

118
00:06:34,647 --> 00:06:38,691
lights, glowing green and red curtains that ripple across

119
00:06:38,691 --> 00:06:39,400
the stars.

120
00:06:39,950 --> 00:06:43,182
Winter crews like to play with the cold, too.

121
00:06:43,182 --> 00:06:46,127
Throw a cup of boiling water into the air

122
00:06:46,127 --> 00:06:49,359
at minus sixty, and it instantly turns into a

123
00:06:49,359 --> 00:06:52,878
cloud of ice crystals that drifts away like snow.

124
00:06:53,428 --> 00:06:57,392
Scientists also drill deep ice cores. Bubbles of ancient

125
00:06:57,392 --> 00:07:00,435
air trapped in the ice tell us what Earth's

126
00:07:00,435 --> 00:07:04,116
climate was like hundreds of thousands of years ago.

127
00:07:04,666 --> 00:07:08,593
Humans need heated buildings to survive. But one animal

128
00:07:08,593 --> 00:07:11,806
does it out in the open: the emperor penguin.

129
00:07:11,806 --> 00:07:14,876
In the middle of winter, males stand on the

130
00:07:14,876 --> 00:07:18,089
ice for about two months, balancing an egg on

131
00:07:18,089 --> 00:07:18,874
their feet.

132
00:07:19,424 --> 00:07:22,762
They don't eat the whole time. To survive the

133
00:07:22,762 --> 00:07:27,286
wind and cold, thousands huddle together, slowly rotating, so

134
00:07:27,286 --> 00:07:30,624
every penguin gets a turn in the warm center.

135
00:07:31,174 --> 00:07:34,782
Weddell seals survive under the sea ice, using their

136
00:07:34,782 --> 00:07:38,321
teeth to keep breathing holes open all winter long.

137
00:07:38,871 --> 00:07:42,938
Under all that ice, Antarctica hides surprises. There are

138
00:07:42,938 --> 00:07:47,147
hundreds of lakes of liquid water buried kilometers beneath

139
00:07:47,147 --> 00:07:51,000
the surface. The biggest, Lake Vostok, has been sealed

140
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,783
off from the air for millions of years.

141
00:07:54,333 --> 00:07:57,586
And the ice holds almost seventy percent of the

142
00:07:57,586 --> 00:08:00,977
world's fresh water. If it all melted, sea levels

143
00:08:00,977 --> 00:08:03,677
would rise by about fifty eight meters.

144
00:08:04,227 --> 00:08:07,764
So, could you survive a winter in Antarctica? On

145
00:08:07,764 --> 00:08:11,302
your own, out in the open, almost certainly not.

146
00:08:11,302 --> 00:08:14,323
The cold, the wind and the dark would get

147
00:08:14,323 --> 00:08:15,576
you within hours.

148
00:08:16,126 --> 00:08:19,381
But inside a station, with a team, warm food

149
00:08:19,381 --> 00:08:22,193
and a reason to be there, people do it

150
00:08:22,193 --> 00:08:25,522
every single year, and many say it's the most

151
00:08:25,522 --> 00:08:29,665
amazing experience of their lives. Then, in late winter,

152
00:08:29,665 --> 00:08:33,143
the first sunrise in months turns the whole sky

153
00:08:33,143 --> 00:08:33,513
gold.

154
00:08:34,063 --> 00:08:37,118
Last question. Where should we go next? Tell me

155
00:08:37,118 --> 00:08:38,159
in the comments.

156
00:08:38,709 --> 00:08:42,478
Thanks for watching Earth Decoded. If you enjoyed this,

157
00:08:42,478 --> 00:08:46,042
like the video, subscribe, and share it with someone

158
00:08:46,042 --> 00:08:49,057
who always says they're cold. See you in the

159
00:08:49,057 --> 00:08:49,674
next one.

